    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Aleppo first appears in tablets of the third millennium BCE, was the capital of the Amorite kingdom of Yamhad, and was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</li><li>Its position on the trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> made it, by the middle of the 16th century, the principal market in the region for goods from the east, with European consulates and the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> Company.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, the borders drawn after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> drew trade and power away from it, and the war that reached the city in 2012 damaged much of its old city.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> captured Aleppo, massacred many of its inhabitants and ordered a tower of 20,000 skulls built outside the city.</p></aside>
    </section>

