    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">3rd millennium BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Aleppo appears for the first time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> tablets</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The kingdom of Yamhad, with Aleppo as its capital, begins to rise to power</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> takes over the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">637</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> Muslims under Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah take Aleppo</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> take the city after a siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1516</span><span class="rf-text">Aleppo becomes part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text">The French declare the State of Aleppo</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2016</span><span class="rf-text">Rebel forces evacuate eastern Aleppo and the Syrian Army declares full control of the city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aleppo lies about 120 km inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>, on a plateau 380 m above sea level. The city was founded a few kilometres south of the present old city, on the right bank of the Queiq River, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a> plateau in the north and runs south to the fertile country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin">Qinnasrin</a>. The old city lies on the left bank. It was surrounded by a circle of eight hills around a prominent central hill, on which a castle was erected that had originally been a temple dating to the 2nd millennium BCE. Because the modern city occupies its ancient site, Aleppo has scarcely been touched by archaeologists.</p><p>The city may have been inhabited since the sixth millennium BCE. Excavations at Tell as-Sawda and Tell al-Ansari, just south of the old city, show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> occupied the area by the latter part of the third millennium BCE. That is also when Aleppo first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, which call it Ha-lam and speak of it as part of the Amorite state of Yamhad. Wayne Horowitz identifies it with Armi, the capital of an independent kingdom closely related to Ebla, but this identification is contested. The main temple of the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> stood on the citadel hill, and the city was known as the city of Hadad. The name Ḥalab is believed to come from a Semitic root meaning milk, and folklore connects it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, who is said to have milked his livestock there to provide for travellers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Yamhad, also known as the land of Ḥalab (c. 1800 to 1525 BCE), was one of the most powerful kingdoms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim I</a>, who allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> made Aleppo a centre of worship of the Storm-God. As Beroea, it was in Late Antiquity the second largest Syrian city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and the population of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> grew under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> well into the 5th century. Under the Hamdanid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>, from 944, it enjoyed a period of great prosperity and was home to the poet al-Mutanabbi and the philosopher al-Farabi. In 1128 it became the capital of the expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who found about 50,000 inhabitants in 1516, Aleppo rose to high prominence because of its position on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the east, at one point being second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the empire. By the middle of the 16th century it had displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as the principal market for goods coming to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, founded in 1581, made it its headquarters until the late 18th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> (1548), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (1562), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1583) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> (1613) opened consulates there. Armenian merchants developed the new quarter of Judayda. The prosperity faded after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> in 1722 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869 hurt the economy further.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Aleppo&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-history and pre-classical era, Medieval period, Historical population, Christians:</p><ul><li>Ross Burns, Aleppo, A History (Routledge, 2016)</li><li>Steven Runciman, A History of the Crusades: Volume 3, The Kingdom of Acre and the Later Crusades (Cambridge University Press, 1987)</li><li>Trevor Bryce, Ancient Syria: A Three Thousand Year History (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li><li>Guy Bunnens, A New Luwian Stele and the Cult of the Storm-god at Til Barsib-Masuwari (Peeters Publishers, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator.jpg" alt="Seleucus I Nicator" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seleukos_I_Nikator_Bronze_Roman_100BCE-100CE_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_Naples_AN_5590_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Gluck, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aleppo was the capital of the Amorite dynasty of Yamhad until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a> devastated it in the 16th century BCE. About 1525 BCE a Mitanni-instigated rebellion ended the life of Yamhad’s last king, Ilim-Ilimma I, and Aleppo came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. Suppiluliumas I conquered it in the 14th century BCE, and a dynasty of his descendants ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>. It was later part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian Empire</a>, of the Aramean realm of Bit Agusi and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, and then passed to the Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and the Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. Alexander took it in 333 BCE, and between 301 and 286 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> Nicator established a Hellenic settlement there, Beroea. Tigranes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 88 BCE, and in 64 BCE it became a Roman province.</p><p>Aleppo was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> Muslims in 637 and was part of Jund <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin">Qinnasrin</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. It became the seat of the Hamdanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in 944 and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 1017, and the Mirdasid dynasty ruled it from 1024 to 1080 with two Fatimid interruptions. Seljuk governors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> (from 1128) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> (from 1183, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>) followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took it in 1260, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> recovered it within a month, and the Mongols took it again in 1271, 1280 and 1299. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> captured it in 1400. It became Ottoman in 1516 and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a> of Aleppo in 1866. In October 1918 Prince Feisal’s forces took it, the French declared the State of Aleppo in September 1920, and in December 1924 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> merged Aleppo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> into a single Syrian State.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Aleppo&#8221; cites where it covers Greek and Roman periods, Islamic period, Pre-history and pre-classical era, Medieval period:</p><ul><li>Ross Burns, Aleppo, A History (Routledge, 2016)</li><li>Steven Runciman, A History of the Crusades: Volume 3, The Kingdom of Acre and the Later Crusades (Cambridge University Press, 1987)</li><li>Trevor Bryce, Ancient Syria: A Three Thousand Year History (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li><li>Guy Bunnens, A New Luwian Stele and the Cult of the Storm-god at Til Barsib-Masuwari (Peeters Publishers, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Because of constant invasions and political instability, the inhabitants built cell-like quarters that were socially and economically independent, each marked by the religion and ethnicity of its people. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a>’s attack in 1400, Christians who had left the city could not resettle in their old quarter, and in 1420 they founded al-Jdayde in the northern suburbs outside the walls; its inhabitants were mainly brokers between foreign traders and local merchants. The old city was enclosed by a wall with nine gates, last rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. Inside lay covered souqs named after crafts such as wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, with khans (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>) that housed traders and their goods and caeserias that served as workshops. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, until the Mongol invasion, the city had 177 hammams.</p><p>Aleppo was a city of several faiths. In the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> recorded a Jewish community of 5,000 people. The Great Synagogue, built in the 5th century, housed the Aleppo Codex, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> joined the community after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. Armenian families and merchants had settled there since at least the 1100s, and the oldest Armenian church dates from 1491. In 1901 the population of 108,143 was 70.58 per cent Muslim, 22.66 per cent Christian, mostly Catholics, and 6.76 per cent Jewish. Armenian and Assyrian refugees arrived after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of 1915. The city’s Arabic is a North Levantine dialect, and Kurdish, Turkish, Armenian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> are also spoken.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="Khosrow I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_the_Sasanian_king_Khosrow_I_Anushirvan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> pillaged and burned Aleppo in 540, and the Byzantine general Nikephoros Phokas sacked it in 962. A deadly earthquake struck on 11 October 1138; the estimates are very unreliable, but it is believed that 230,000 people died. In January 1260 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>, with Frankish and Armenian allies, took the city: the walls fell after six days of siege and the citadel four weeks later. The Muslim population was massacred and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were killed, while the Christian population was spared. In 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> massacred many inhabitants and ordered a tower of 20,000 skulls built outside the city.</p><p>According to Sheikh Kamel Al-Ghazzi the population was around 400,000 before the earthquake of 1822. Cholera followed in 1823, and around 20 to 25 percent of the population died of plague in 1827. In 1850 a Muslim mob attacked Christian neighbourhoods, killing tens of Christians and looting several churches.</p><p>In August 2011 security forces shot and killed at least twelve people at anti-government protests in Aleppo. Fighting reached the city in July 2012, and by spring 2013 the Syrian Army held the west and the Free Syrian Army the east. Many parts of the Al-Madina Souq and other medieval buildings were destroyed, ruined or burnt, and the tower of the Great Umayyad Mosque was destroyed in March 2013. In 2016 government forces closed the last supply line into the east, the rebels evacuated in December, and on 22 December the army declared full control. An estimated 31,000 people were killed in Aleppo during the conflict.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old City, the walled city together with the old quarters outside it, covers about 350 ha. It holds covered souqs, khans, hammams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, mosques and churches in the styles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a> stands on a mound rising 50 m above the city, and the Great Umayyad Mosque was founded about 715 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Walid I. In 1954 the French architect André Gutton proposed wide new roads, and between 1954 and 1983 many old buildings were demolished. His plan was abandoned in 1979 for one by Stefano Bianca that preserved the traditional urban fabric, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> placed the Old City on the World Heritage List in 1986.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Aleppo lost its northern hinterland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and in 1939 it lost its main access to the sea when Alexandretta was annexed to Turkey. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> grew in importance and became the capital. In 2011 the Ancient Villages of Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the Dead Cities near Aleppo, were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. On 29 November 2024 Syrian opposition groups led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayat-tahrir-al-sham">Hayat Tahrir al-Sham</a> captured the city. Aleppo is today the capital of the Aleppo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, with a population of roughly two million, and reconstruction of the parts damaged in the war is under way.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/katibim">Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken)<span>1850</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Aleppo, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, first appears in tablets of the third millennium BCE and was the capital of the Amorite kingdom of Yamhad. It was ruled in turn by many powers and was a leading trading city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The war of 2012 to 2016 damaged much of its old city.</em></p>

